  "analysis": "We need to construct a timetable B = [b1, b2, ..., bn] given departure times A = [a1, a2, ..., an], travel time t, and the maximum arrival positions X = [x1, x2, ..., xn] for each bus. The problem is from Codeforces (Timetable). A solution must satisfy that for each i: bi must be strictly increasing, and the i-th bus (departing at ai) can arrive no later than x_i, with the property that x_i is the maximum possible arrival position across all valid permutations (given B). This is a tricky constructive problem with a known approach: (i) the sequence X should be nondecreasing in i and xi >= i for feasibility in typical constructions; (ii) we construct b in a way that ensures there is at least one valid permutation and that for each i, there exists a valid permutation with bus i arriving at position x_i. A widely used constructive approach is to enforce that for each k, b_k is large enough to accommodate all buses whose x_i <= k, while maintaining strict increase. A simple and robust way to implement this is:
- Validate that X is nondecreasing and xi >= i for all i; otherwise, answer is No.
- Compute c_i = a_i + t for all i.
- For each k in 1..n, compute need[k] = max c_i over all i with x_i = k; and pref[k] = max(pref[k-1], need[k]).
- Then set b1 = max(pref[1], 1) and for k > 1 set bk = max(pref[k], b{k-1} + 1). This guarantees strict increase and that for any i with x_i = k, bk >= c_i, so bus i can be placed at position k in some valid permutation. It also ensures at least one valid permutation (by construction, if we also satisfy the xi >= i condition). If these conditions fail, we output No. Finally, print Yes and the b array.
Note: While this approach is a common constructive method, it’s important to realize that edge cases may require more nuanced handling. The given constraints and structure (n up to 2e5, t, a_i up to 1e18) make using 128-bit arithmetic prudent for sums, but a_i + t fits in signed 64-bit here (max around 2e18). The solution operates in O(n).",
